1. Summary and Technical Explanation

The /forgot-password endpoint returns the password reset link directly in the application’s response (rendered on the page) instead of only delivering it through the registered email address. This means the reset flow does not require any access to the victim’s inbox at all — anyone who submits a target’s email address to the endpoint is handed a working reset token by the server itself.

Normally the reset token is a secret shared only with the account owner via a channel they control (their email). By echoing the link back in the HTTP response/page body, the server breaks that trust boundary: possession of the token no longer proves the requester owns the email address. Any unauthenticated attacker can request a reset for any known or guessed email, read the token straight off the page, and use it to set a new password — resulting in full account takeover with no interaction from the victim and no email access required.

This also makes the issue trivially scriptable: an attacker can iterate over a list of email addresses (harvested, leaked, or enumerated) and take over accounts in bulk.

2. Steps to Reproduce
Identify a target account’s registered email address (e.g., a known user, or an address enumerated via the registration/login flow).
Navigate to https://geo-salmon-complement-walk.trycloudflare.com/forgot-password.
Submit the target’s email address in the “forgot password” form.
Observe the server’s response: the password reset link (containing the reset token) is displayed directly on the page, rather than being sent exclusively to the target’s inbox.
Copy the disclosed link and open it.
Set a new password for the target’s account.
Log in using the target’s email and the newly set password.

Expected result: The response confirms only that an email was sent, with no token or link exposed to the requester.

Actual result: The reset link/token is returned in the response body and can be used immediately to take over any account, without ever touching the victim’s email.

3. Impact
Unauthenticated full account takeover for any user whose email address is known or can be guessed/enumerated — no victim interaction needed.
Mass exploitation: the flow can be automated to take over many accounts by iterating a list of emails.
Complete bypass of the “something you have” (email access) factor that the reset mechanism is meant to rely on.
Downstream impact depends on what the account controls — data exposure, financial transactions, further pivoting if the account has elevated privileges, etc.
4. Remediation
Never return the reset token or link in the API/page response. The response to a forgot-password request must be generic (e.g., “If this email exists, a reset link has been sent”) regardless of whether the account exists or the token was generated.
Deliver the token only via the verified email channel, never via the HTTP response, logs, or any client-visible output.
Rate-limit and monitor the forgot-password endpoint to detect and slow down enumeration/abuse attempts.
Use a uniform response time and message for both existing and non-existing emails to prevent user enumeration through this same endpoint.
Pair this fix with single-use, short-lived tokens (see the companion report on token reuse) so that even a leaked token has a narrow exploitation window.
Audit logs and client-side code/network traffic to confirm no other endpoint leaks reset tokens (e.g., via response headers, JS console logs, or analytics beacons).
